    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d668">

                <pb facs="1226" n="1204" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg1204"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">783.56/1–1050: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Legation in Syria </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-01-27T19:00:00-05:00">January 27, 1950—7
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>40. Legtel 20, Jan 10. Dept approves ur comments to PriMin. No legal auth now
                    exists provide mil mission or grant or sell Syria arms from USG procurement.
                    However Dept contemplates no change vis-à-vis Syria its export licensing pol
                    Arab States and Israel defined Depcirtel Sep 7, 1949.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d668-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p> FYI: Although policy above wld permit Syrians purchase arms US commercial
                    sources not likely quantities available commercial dealers cld materially affect
                    Syrian econ or mil position. Moreover all applications export licenses carefully
                    screened in terms limitations set forth refcirtel.</p>
                <p> FYI: Conversations with Brit progressing on exchange info arms shipments NE
                    states. Talks not yet initiated with ER this subj pending estab working
                    agreement with UK. Meanwhile Dept wld welcome ur further comments and summary
                    available info Fr arms shipments Syria to date and projected.</p>
                <p> FYI: Syrian Leg notified Dept Jan 18 that any arms export application made prior
                    that date does not carry approval Syrian Govt. Accordingly, application export
                    Dec 2 to Syria Minister of Natl. Defense spare parts for AT–6 value $248,000.00
                    and application pending since Aug 1949 for export 10,000 steel helmets being
                    cancelled by Dept.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d668-f1" n="1" xml:id="d668-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it stated that the United States would export arms to a country if
                    it were determined that the equipment was necessary for “(<hi
                        rendition="#italic">a</hi>) maintaining internal law and order by Govt in
                    reasonable and legitimate exercise of constituted authority; (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) reasonable requirement of self defense.”
                    (711.00111 Armament Control/9–749).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
